Pinecone API v2 quickstart additions

Retrying a paginated GraphQL request

If a paginated GraphQL request returns 401 because the JWT expired, refresh the JWT and replay the same request with the same cursor. Do not restart pagination from the previously successful page.

    query ListItems($cursor: String) {
      items(after: $cursor) {
        nodes { id }
        pageInfo { endCursor hasNextPage }
      }
    }

    # On JWT expiry:
    # 1. Refresh the JWT.
    # 2. Replay the request with the original $cursor.

Retrying a mutation rejected before resolver execution

If a mutation returns 401 before resolver execution, treat the failed request as having no side effect. After refreshing the JWT, the client may retry the same mutation.

    mutation UpdateItem($id: ID!, $value: String!) {
      updateItem(id: $id, value: $value) {
        id
      }
    }

Unsupported token-rotation headers

Token-rotation headers are unsupported and are outside the current GraphQL/JWT connector path.

Proposed replacement clause:

Connector Support; Limited Remediation Commitment

1. Company will provide and maintain documentation for the connectors identified in the Documentation as supported connectors (“Supported Connectors”) and will provide support for Supported Connectors in accordance with the applicable Support Policy. Company will use commercially reasonable efforts to investigate and remediate any reproducible defect in the Company platform or a Supported Connector that is within Company’s reasonable control.

2. If Company confirms that a connection failure or unavailability is caused solely by a reproducible defect in the Company platform or a Supported Connector, Company will use commercially reasonable efforts to provide a reasonable workaround within two (2) Business Days after such confirmation, provided that Customer has supplied all information, access, logs, credentials, and cooperation reasonably necessary for the investigation. The foregoing is a limited remediation commitment and does not constitute a warranty of uninterrupted connectivity, a guarantee of successful setup, or a commitment to provide a workaround where no technically reasonable workaround is available.

3. The workaround timing commitment in Section 2, and any service credit or other service-level remedy, will not apply to any failure, delay, degradation, or unavailability caused in whole or in part by:

   a. Customer’s permissions, credentials, configuration, access controls, network settings, security policies, or failure to provide required access or cooperation;

   b. any change, outage, deprecation, restriction, rate limit, or other action or condition of the applicable source system or its operator;

   c. a connector, source, integration, feature, or configuration that is not identified in the Documentation as a Supported Connector;

   d. Customer’s setup error, omission, misuse, or failure to follow the Documentation or Company’s reasonable instructions;

   e. inaccurate, incomplete, malformed, or incompatible data supplied by Customer or the source system;

   f. an outage or failure of a third-party system or service outside Company’s reasonable control; or

   g. any force majeure event or other circumstance outside Company’s reasonable control.

4. Company may reasonably suspend or extend any applicable remediation period while awaiting Customer information, access, approvals, corrective action, or other cooperation. If a failure has multiple causes, the exclusions in Section 3 apply to the extent the failure is caused or contributed to by an excluded cause.

5. No service credit, fee reduction, refund, or other financial remedy will be due for any event described in Section 3. A workaround provided by Company is not an agreement to support an unsupported connector, does not expand the scope of the Supported Connectors, and does not create any obligation to deliver equivalent functionality through a manual or alternative integration.

Suggested cover note to Legal:

“This narrows the two-business-day commitment to confirmed, reproducible defects in documented supported connectors or the platform, while preserving reasonable efforts to remediate those defects. It expressly removes customer-permission issues, source-system changes, unsupported connectors, and customer setup errors from both the workaround clock and any credit remedy, and makes clear that the clause does not guarantee setup success or uninterrupted connectivity.”
